s $8F2F Unused
D $8F2F Zero padding up to the start of the opening book at $9000.
@ $9000 label=BOOK_DATA
b $9000 Opening book
D $9000 A pre-order tree of 2685 9-bit entries (24165 bits, $9000-$9BCC), read by #R$7B64 and walked by #R$7AC7. Entry n occupies bit-stream bits 9n to 9n+8 (most significant bit first); each DEFB line below holds 9 bytes, i.e. entries 8k to 8k+7, where k is the line number counting from 0.
D $9000 Entry layout, from the most significant bit: bit 8 'M' (on the first entry of a sibling list it raises that move's chance of being chosen from 1/2 to 3/4; on any later entry it stops the random choice before that entry, so it and all later siblings are never chosen, only followed when the opponent plays them); bit 7 = leaf (no stored continuation); bit 6 = more siblings follow; bits 0-5 = index of the move in the list produced by the move generator #R$78CE for that position (move = $B404+4*index). A non-leaf entry is followed immediately by the sibling list of its replies. The root list (entry 0) holds White's first moves: 1.e4, 1.d4, 1.c4, 1.Nf3, 1.b3 (M), 1.f4 (M).
D $9000 The decoded tree is in docs/arch/book-tree.txt (generated by tools/scripts/book_dump.py): 396 leaves, 123 entries with M set, maximum depth 31 plies.
B $9000,3021,9 Entries 0-2684
u $9BCD Unused
D $9BCD Data after the end of the book tree. It is never read: every walk through the book stays within entries 0-2684 (bytes $9000-$9BCC). The first 52 bytes do not parse as a continuation of the tree (probably left over from development).
B $9BCD,52,16 Stale data
N $9C01 From here to the end the bytes follow the repeating pattern $FF,$FF,$01,$01 (a typical power-on RAM pattern, captured when the tape was mastered), apart from 18 isolated bytes that differ (e.g. $9C10=$EB, $9C20=$B3, $9E00=$DB).
B $9C01,1023,16 Uninitialised memory
